<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Pre-existing gap found while wiring task 10.4's local-network config:
         this was declared only in src/debug and src/profile's manifests (the
         stock `flutter create` template's assumption that a fresh project has
         no network feature), so a release build had NO network access at
         all — map tiles, DEM fetches, and any future server sync would all
         silently fail. Belongs here, in every build type, not layered
         per-variant like the network-security-config below. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <!-- Task 11.40. **The same gap as the comment above, found again, and this
         time it had been live since Phase 3.** `lib/core/providers/gps_provider.dart`
         calls `Geolocator.requestPermission()` and nothing on Android had ever
         declared a location permission, so Android denied it **without showing
         a dialog** — `requestPermission()` returns `denied` immediately, the
         provider throws `LocationPermissionDeniedException`, and the app shows
         its own tidy "location was denied" message. A person would read that as
         having been asked and having said no. It is the shape task 11.38 found
         in the macOS keychain: the OS refuses, the app has a graceful path for
         the refusal, and the graceful path is exactly what hides the fact that
         nobody was ever asked.

         The comment above fixed the INTERNET instance in task 10.4 and did not
         derive the class, which is why this one survived. What derives it now
         is `test/platform/capability_matrix_test.dart` — every capability the
         app's Dart code requests must have a row in
         `CLAUDE_DOCS/CAPABILITY_MATRIX.md` naming where it is declared.

         COARSE as well as FINE: an Android 12+ user may grant only approximate
         location from the system dialog, and a bearing recorded from an
         approximate fix is still worth more than no observation. `LocationAccuracy.high`
         asks for the precise one; this pair is what makes the dialog appear at all.

         **Declared, not verified.** Nobody has run this app on an Android
         device. See `CLAUDE_DOCS/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md` item H2. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION"/>
    <application
        android:label="TriTrack"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <!-- Task 10.79, finding L-10, turned Android Auto Backup off: with no
             `allowBackup` attribute it defaulted to true, so the whole app
             sandbox — including `tri_track.db` and every coordinate in it —
             was copied to the user's Google account on its own schedule, with
             no TriTrack decision involved. That reasoning was sound as far as
             it went, but it traded away Auto Backup's safety net without
             asking whether the trade was worth it.

             Task 11.215 put it back, on Michael's decision, 2026-09-10: a
             phone lost in a canyon is far likelier than a stolen cloud
             account, and the records at risk are the same set either way —
             the ones not yet on the server. For a field app used for days at
             a time with no signal, that redundancy is worth more than the
             exposure, for essentially every project. L-10 in
             `TriTrack_Security_Model.md` records this as an accepted risk,
             not a closed one, along with the remedy for the rare project that
             cannot accept it: a person can turn backup off for this app alone
             in the phone's own settings (iOS has always had this; Android
             gained it in the June 2026 per-app backup control rollout). Do
             not re-add `allowBackup="false"` without re-reading that
             finding. -->

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Task 10.77 (finding K-3): the activity above is exported
                 (required to launch at all), and an exported activity is
                 startable by any app on the device with an explicit
                 component intent regardless of intent-filters — Flutter's
                 embedding then reads that intent's `route` extra
                 unconditionally and hands it to go_router as the initial
                 route, bypassing splash_screen.dart's identity gate. This
                 stops the embedding from honoring that extra at all;
                 app_router.dart's `overridePlatformDefaultLocation` is the
                 second, platform-independent layer. -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="flutter_deeplinking_enabled"
                android:value="false" />
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
